---
name: dev-fix
version: 1.13.0
description: lead dev-fix assignment for a dev bead reopened after a failed sanity check. Same bead (task_id); its open child finding beads are the work, fixed on a new layer cut from the stack's current top.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - pr_target
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
optional_variables:
  - sanity_bead
defaults:
  sanity_bead: ""
metadata:
  type: dev-fix
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" mode="dev-fix">
  <description><![CDATA[{% if sanity_bead %}Sanity check `{{ sanity_bead | string | cdata_escape }}` failed dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` and reopened it.{% else %}The task assigner reopened dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` after plan review.{% endif %} Its open child finding beads are the work. `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is still the assignment.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[The sprint's first layer `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` and any fix layer above it are linked and frozen: never rebase, re-target or push them. `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is a new layer cut from the top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` at dispatch; commit the fixes there. Its PR lands on the current top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}`. Never edit another branch or run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin` in the worktree (no rebase: the branch was cut from the stack top, and check (6) below, `--is-ancestor`, proves it holds `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Each check, its command and its refusal code: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) difficulty: your `atm members --json` model is in `DIFFICULTY_MODELS[metadata.difficulty]`, else `DIFFICULTY_MISMATCH`; (6) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`. `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. Reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. On success claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it or start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee; still `closed` means never reopened), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (its open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each. Then refuse; never wait: close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`, `bead_state` `open`, `reason_md` giving the bead, why it is not ready, which bead or agent must move, and, for a blocker not yet its dependency, the edge to add, `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`, so `bd ready` lists the bead only once the blocker closes. The task assigner re-assigns it then.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` and its open child beads (`bd list --parent {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --json`). Read the complete plan and enumerate every task to complete in the primary and child beads; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time, closing each child when fixed: `bd close <child> --reason "<short sha>: <what fixed it>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`. Then go through the checklist again one item at a time, confirming each is done, and re-check the whole bead: the sanity check reports what it saw, not everything missing. Never weaken a test or criterion to clear a finding. The bead's `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` still govern the fix: re-read each one named.]]></step>
    <step id="b1"><![CDATA[If the work needs a change other live branches need at the same time (a shared type or trait signature, or a critical bug every branch carries), stop that edit, tell the task assigner the exact change and the branches it breaks, follow the assigner's base (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md`, Parallel Quick Fix), and report a quick-fix PR you open by plain `atm send`.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`; the top is what `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py stack-top --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` prints on exit 0 (from GitHub's stacks); any other exit prints a code (`STACK_AMBIGUOUS`, `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`): never guess, refuse with it by step e1; then `git rebase origin/<top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Open the PR against that top: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --fill` (never `--draft`), run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Run `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "dev complete at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then, only if it succeeded, `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/dev-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Vars: `rebased_onto`, `pr_number`, `pr_url` and `stack_view` from step d; `deliverables_md` lists each finding and its fix. The sanity check then reruns on the new head. Only this close frees your queue.]]></step>
    <step id="e1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; create no per-task shadow and do not delay the refusal. Announce a serious infrastructure failure once per cause to the oversight recipients (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md` Lead Role). Declare failure with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "DEV_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. A blocker is not a failure: when the task needs unfinished work another bead or agent owns (a prerequisite open or reopened, another agent's fix not landed), stop, return the bead with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "BLOCKED: <blocker>: <why>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and close the task `refused` with the same command (`bead_state` `open`), its `reason_md` naming the blocker and the edge `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`; never stay active waiting. A shared change you can make yourself is not a blocker: it is a Parallel Quick Fix (step b1) and the task continues. Only a genuine cannot-complete uses `--status blocked`.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
